Fisher D-optimal item selection evaluated at upper-tail reference abilities; target ability by MAP from k answers; linear model-assisted difference estimator and calibrated-mean starter as stack members; non-negative least-squares stacking weights fit only on source pseudo-target folds (strongest-holdout folds for the strong-target regime) with fallback to simpler estimators.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Linear model-assisted D-optimal estimator (search variant c01)\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Earlier, simpler configuration (search utility 0.9381) that the stack contains as a fallback member; serves as a within-family reference showing what logistic modelling/Fisher design/stacking added, with the caveat that it is a cumulative-change comparison on 2 search seeds, not a clean single-factor ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Bagged posterior-mean + tail-calibration extension (search variant c03)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Negative result: added a fourth stack candidate for strong targets but did not improve over the stack (0.9445 vs 0.9452; MAE strong 0.0362 vs 0.0359; Spearman strong 0.812 vs 0.816). Reported briefly as evidence that further tail correction on source pseudo-targets did not help.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Trusted baselines: random subset mean, k-means anchor points, IRT-style estimator\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Evaluator-owned matched comparators at the same k=100 on the same episodes; random subset mean is the best baseline by utility (delta vs best baseline equals delta vs random mean). Final-evaluation MAE improvements: 0.0045 vs random mean, 0.0134 vs anchor points, 0.0398 vs IRT-style (positive = candidate better; CI available only for the best-baseline/random-mean comparison).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Negative controls (permuted targets, constant targets)\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Show that predictions depend on the target's revealed answers (permuted-targets Spearman drop 1.157, MAE increase 0.156; constant-targets MAE delta 0.094 in final) so the reported accuracy is not a protocol artifact.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Held-out final comparison (5 disjoint benchmarks, 3 seeds, k=100): candidate MAE 0.0294, RMSE 0.0354, Spearman 0.843, Kendall 0.715, utility 0.9314 versus baselines; MAE deltas +0.0045 (random mean), +0.0134 (anchor points), +0.0398 (IRT-style); paired hierarchical bootstrap (benchmarks, seeds, target models; 300 replicates, 95% percentile interval) for the best-baseline gap: MAE [-0.0052, 0.0134], utility [-0.0143, 0.0354] -- both include zero, so state 'improvement not statistically resolved'.\",\n    \"Regime breakdown on the final evaluation: random targets MAE 0.0179 / Spearman 0.956 versus strong targets (stronger than every source; sources are below the 0.7 quantile of model score) MAE 0.0409 / Spearman 0.730; same pattern on search benchmarks for all three variants (strong-target MAE roughly 2x random-target MAE). Per-regime baseline numbers are not in the artifacts: do not claim where the gain over baselines is concentrated.\",\n    \"Negative-control sanity checks (final): permuted and constant targets degrade the prediction as expected.\",\n    \"Within-family variant comparison on search benchmarks (2 seeds, all 3 of 3 variants scored, 0 unscored): utility 0.9381 -> 0.9452 -> 0.9445; strong-target Spearman 0.771 -> 0.816 -> 0.812; difference between the two top variants (0.0006) is smaller than the seed-to-seed spread within a variant (e.g. 0.9423 vs 0.9480), so the choice of c02 over c03 is not statistically supported; c01->c02 gain (+0.0071) is larger but still from 2 seeds.\",\n    \"Search-to-final shift: utility 0.9452 (search, 5 development benchmarks, 2 seeds) vs 0.9314 (final, 5 disjoint benchmarks, 3 seeds); do not attribute the drop to a cause (different benchmarks and seeds); report as measured and keep search and final in separate tables/panels.\",\n    \"Scope statement: single budget k=100, binary accuracy only, one target-regime definition, 5+5 benchmarks; no sweep over k, no ablations beyond the three variants.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Fig. 1 (overview, page 1-2): prediction protocol and method at a glance -- source binary matrix -> logistic latent fit -> Fisher D-optimal selection of k=100 items -> target answers on selected items -> stacked estimator with source pseudo-target validation and fallback -> predicted full accuracy; side inset contrasting the two target regimes (random vs stronger than all sources).\",\n    \"Fig. 2 (main result, held-out final benchmarks): panel A overall MAE of candidate vs random subset mean, anchor points, IRT-style (baseline MAE obtained as candidate MAE + reported delta, labelled derived); panel B paired improvement vs the best baseline (MAE and utility) with 95% bootstrap intervals that visibly cross zero.\",\n    \"Fig. 3 (extrapolation gap): MAE and Spearman for random vs strong targets, shown for development (search) and held-out (final) benchmark groups in separate, clearly labelled categories.\",\n    \"Table 1: final-evaluation metrics (MAE, RMSE, Spearman, Kendall, utility), per-seed utility (0.9257, 0.9324, 0.9360), controls. Table 2: search-stage variant comparison with per-seed utilities and strong/random breakdown.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_protocol\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give immediate glance value: the few-item prediction setting (source matrix, k selected items, target answers, full-score prediction) and the proposed pipeline, plus the two target regimes.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
