odel by a cluster-size-weighted average over representative items. tinyBenchmarks \\citep{Polo2024tinyBenchmarksEL} combines such anchors with item response theory (IRT) to predict full scores, and metabench \\citep{Kipnis2024metabenchA} uses IRT-based information to select items and fits a predictor on top of them. Efficient benchmarking \\citep{Perlitz2023EfficientB} studies how much a large benchmark can be reduced while preserving model rankings, and PromptEval \\citep{Polo2024EfficientME} applies related methods to the variation introduced by prompt formats. ONEBench \\citep{Ghosh2024ONEBenchTT} studies how to aggregate sparse sample-level measurements into model scores and rankings. Our baselines follow the clustering and IRT families; the baseline implementations are the evaluator's own re-implementations rather than the original code.\n62:\\paragraph{Learned and adaptive prediction.} Pacchiardi et al.\\ \\citep{Pacchiardi2024100II} train an assessor that predicts a new model's success on unseen instances from its results on a few reference instances. Active evaluation acquisition \\citep{Li2024ActiveEA} learns a policy that chooses items adaptively and predicts the rest. Adaptive testing in the psychometric tradition \\citep{Zhuang2023PositionAE} and its extension to continuous scores \\citep{Balkir2026ConfidentRW} choose items sequentially with stopping rules. Our setting is static: the $k$ items are chosen before seeing the target, from source data alone. A comparison of eleven benchmark-prediction methods across many benchmarks is reported by Zhang et al.\\ \\citep{Zhang2025HowBP}, whose released per-item score matrices we use as data (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}); we make no claim about their methods or conclusions beyond what the matrices contain.\n64:\\paragraph{Estimation and design tools.} The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model \\citep{Rodriguez2021EvaluationEA} fitted to a model-by-item matrix. The final estimator follows the model-assisted approach of correcting a model-based prediction with an observed-sample residual, in the spirit of prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI}. Item selection by greedy log-determinant maximization is D-optimal experimental design \\citep{Kiefer1960TheEO}, and the combination of estimators with non-negative weights fitted on held-out predictions is stacked generalization \\citep{Wolpert1992StackedG}. Benchmark scores themselves vary with seeds and prompts \\citep{Madaan2024QuantifyingVI}, which motivates reporting uncertainty for the gain over simple baselines.\n66:\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} The data comprise per-item outcomes on HELM-derived and Open LLM Leaderboard-derived benchmarks \\citep{Liang2022HolisticEO}: development benchmarks IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023InstructionFollowingEF}, ARC-Challenge \\citep{Clark2018ThinkYH}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023MuSRTT}, GSM8K \\citep{Cobbe2021TrainingVT} and BBH \\citep{Suzgun2022ChallengingBT}; and held-out benchmarks GPQA \\citep{Rein2023GPQAAG}, MedQA \\citep{Jin2020WhatDD}, LegalBench \\citep{Guha2023LegalBenchAC}, MMLU \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM} and MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUProAM}.\n68:\\section{Setting and Method}\n70:\\subsection{Problem setup}\n73:\\subsection{Design rationale}\n79:\\subsection{Selected estimator}\n93:\\subsection{Compared approaches}\n98:\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n100:\\paragraph{Data.} Binary per-item outcome matrices (models $\\times$ items) come from the public per-item score release accompanying \\citet{Zhang2025HowBP}, built from the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM. Five benchmarks were available for development (IFEval, ARC-Challenge, MuSR, GSM8K, BBH) and five disjoint benchmarks were reserved for the sealed final evaluation (GPQA, MedQA, LegalBench, MMLU, MMLU-Pro). The development evaluation uses seeds 11 and 12 (22 episodes including two controls); the final evaluation uses seeds 9101--9103 (32 episodes including two controls). Item order is randomly permuted per benchmark and seed. The target count is the larger of 5 and 20\\% of the models; every episode requires at least 40 sources.\n108:\\section{Results}\n110:\\subsection{Held-out comparison with baselines}\n153:\\subsection{Extrapolation to targets stronger than all sources}\n165:\\subsection{Negative controls}\n169:\\subsection{Within-family variants on development benchmarks}\n189:\\subsection{Search versus held-out performance}\n193:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n210:\\section{Conclusion}\n221:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n223:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol and metric definitions}\n226:\\subsection{Estimator details of the selected configuration}\n237:\\subsection{Development-benchmark results for all three configurations}\n263:\\subsection{Final evaluation details}\n267:\\subsection{Reproducibility, data provenance and seeds}\n268:\\textbf{Data.} The per-item matrices are the public score files released with \\citet{Zhang2025HowBP}, pinned to a fixed repository commit (55faff49\\ldots{}), under an MIT license, derived from the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM. \\textbf{Seeds.} Development 11, 12; final 9101, 9102, 9103; bootstrap 20260101; the evaluated configuration seeds its own generators from the episode seed. \\textbf{Software.} The task environment pins numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, scikit-learn 1.9.1 and joblib 1.6.0; the evaluated configuration runs on CPU with eight BLAS threads. \\textbf{Figures.} Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and \\ref{fig:gap} are produced by a single plotting script from the evaluator outputs and the retained metrics files; the overview figure is a schematic and contains no data. \\textbf{Code.} The numbers derive from a single-file estimator and the evaluator scripts, whose baselines are the evaluator's own implementations; they are not linked in this anonymized draft. We did not access the raw leaderboard details, which are partly gated.\n270:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
