ither random held-out models or models stronger than every source.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Problem setup}\nFor a benchmark with $n$ items, let $S\\in\\{0,1\\}^{m\\times n}$ be the binary correctness matrix of $m$ source models. A \\emph{target} model is a new model whose per-item results are hidden except on a chosen subset. The procedure has two steps. A \\emph{selector} sees only $S$ (with opaque item indices) and returns $k{=}100$ distinct item indices. The evaluator then reveals the targets' answers $Y\\in\\{0,1\\}^{t\\times k}$ on those items only, and a \\emph{predictor} returns a predicted full-benchmark accuracy $\\hat y_j$ for each target $j$. The reference is the target's true full accuracy $y_j$. Per episode we compute MAE, RMSE, Spearman and Kendall correlation between $\\hat y$ and $y$ across the targets of that episode. Targets are drawn in one of two regimes: \\emph{random targets} (a random 20\\% of models, sources are the rest) and \\emph{strong targets} (targets drawn from models whose full accuracy is at or above the 0.7 quantile; sources are the models strictly below it, so every target exceeds every source). The regime label is supplied to the estimator with the source data; baselines ignore it.\n\n\\subsection{Design rationale}\nThe configuration is motivated by three observations; we state them as motivation, not as demonstrated mechanisms, because no single-factor ablation was run (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n(i) Binary outcomes saturate: a linear latent model can predict probabilities outside $[0,1]$ and must be clipped, whereas a logistic model keeps item probabilities calibrated and naturally describes how an item discriminates between abilities.\n(ii) Under a logistic model an item's contribution to the information about a target's ability is $p(1-p)\\,a a^{\\top}$, which is largest near $p=0.5$. Evaluating this at reference abilities in the \\emph{upper tail} of the source abilities, rather than at the